UNIT encode_value
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@7b348e351c9f cli/sir/canon.mjs (unchanged since bb8ded84fa50) — encode(value, parseType(type)) with no options (the neutral codec), as
  composed by selfhost/seams/encode_value.mjs; the code is the truth, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG encode_value(value: json, type: text) -> json
ENVELOPE value is any json tree (numbers include NaN, the infinities and -0), including the reserved shapes R2 and R4
  give meaning to; type is any text. Throws are IN: a type text outside the grammar, a malformed reserved shape (R4), a
  value past the nesting cap (R13) and a value that does not fit its type all throw. The js file widens value to
  host-only kinds (an absent value, arbitrary-precision integers, byte strings, keyed collections with non-text keys,
  text holding an unpaired surrogate, lists with holes).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION The sir-canonical-v1 encoder: parse an abstract SIR type, then turn a value into the canonical tagged tree for
  that type, or throw. Every node of the result is a map with a member t naming its kind (TABLE NODES); members v, cu and
  bits carry the payload. The member order of a node is immaterial (results are compared canonically); list order is
  significant.
BEHAVIOR
R1 TYPE GRAMMAR. The type text is parsed completely before the value is looked at; any failure throws, whatever the
  value. The text must be exactly one TYPE, where TYPE is one ALT or two or more ALTs joined by "|" (a union, in written
  order), and ALT is one of: a scalar name from TABLE NODES (text bytes bool none i64 u64 f64 json); "tuple[" TYPE
  ("," TYPE)* "]" (one or more items); "list[" TYPE "]" (exactly one); "map[" TYPE "," TYPE "]" (exactly two);
  "record[" FIELD ("," FIELD)* "]" with FIELD = NAME ":" TYPE (one or more fields, names distinct). A NAME is a letter or
  "_" followed by letters, digits or "_" (ASCII only), and a word is always read greedily, so "textx" is an unknown name.
  Space, tab, carriage return and line feed may appear between ANY two tokens (a keyword and its "[" included) and at
  both ends — no other character is whitespace. Names are case-sensitive. So "text|", "text || none", "list[]",
  "record[]", "map[text]", "list[f64, text]", "record[text]", "record[0: none]" and "text none" all throw, while
  " record [ a : list [ f64 ] ] " parses. Composite types (tuple, list, map, record) nest at most MAX_DEPTH deep: a
  composite with MAX_DEPTH composites around it throws (16 nested lists around text parse, 17 throw); siblings do not
  add up (a tuple of two 15-deep lists is 16 deep).
R2 UNREACHABLE ALTERNATIVES. A union (at any depth) throws the parse when a later alternative is COVERED by any earlier
  one of the same union. COVERS(a, b) — earlier a, later b — holds exactly when ANY of these holds:
  (a) SAME: a and b are the same type, where a union compares as the SET of its alternatives and a record as the SET of
  its named fields (orders immaterial; names and field types count), every other composite position by position;
  (b) a is json and b is JSON-REACHED: none, bool, f64, text or json; a list of a JSON-REACHED item; a tuple whose items
  are all JSON-REACHED; a map whose key type is exactly the scalar text (not a union holding it) and whose value type is
  JSON-REACHED; a record whose field types are all JSON-REACHED; a union of JSON-REACHED alternatives;
  (c) STRUCTURAL: both lists and COVERS(item, item); both tuples of the same length and COVERS item by item; both maps
  and COVERS on the key types and on the value types; both records and EVERY field of a has a field of the same name in
  b that it COVERS (b may have more fields, a may not);
  (d) b is a union and a COVERS every alternative of b; or a is a union and some alternative of a COVERS b.
  Nothing else counts (a record never covers a map, a list never a tuple, json never bytes, i64 or u64). So
  "none | none", "json | none", "json | tuple[text]", "json | record[a: text]", "list[json] | list[text]",
  "list[text | json] | list[json]", "map[text, json] | map[text, text]" and "record[a: text] | record[a: text, b: none]"
  throw, while "none | json", "json | i64", "json | tuple[i64]", "json | map[json, f64]",
  "json | map[text | none, f64]", "list[text] | list[text | none]", "tuple[json, json] | tuple[text]",
  "record[a: text, b: none] | record[a: text]" and "tuple[text, none] | tuple[none, text]" parse.
  NOTE coverage is decided on types alone: an alternative R2 refuses may still be the only one that accepts some value
  (the absent value under none, a record's unread members) — the parse refuses it all the same (FIDELITY package).
R3 THROW OBSERVATIONS. At every TYPED position — the top, each union alternative, each tuple or list item, each map key
  and value, each record field, and the top of a json position — the value is first tested, before the type is
  consulted: a map with EXACTLY ONE member, named "__throw" or "__threw", whose value is text encodes as the node t throw
  with msg = that text. A second member of any kind makes it an ordinary map ({"__throw": "x", "a": 1} is a map, and
  {"__t": "num", "v": "5", "__throw": "x"} is the num tag of R5); a non-text marker value is an ordinary member. Only a
  map is ever a throw observation (never a list), and an ESCAPED map (R5) never is. Inside json content (below the top
  of a json position) such maps are ordinary maps.
R4 ABSENT. The absent value arises from a record field the value lacks (R8) or an undef tag (R5). It encodes as t none
  where the type is none or a union with a none alternative, and throws everywhere else — json included.
R5 LEGACY TAGS. After R3, a map holding a text member "__t" (and not ESCAPED) is a v0.4 codec tag and is replaced by the
  value it stands for before the type is applied — at every typed position, and at every node inside json content:
  num: member v must be text: "NaN", "Infinity", "-Infinity" or "-0" give those numbers; otherwise v must spell a json
  number literal (optional "-", then "0" or a digit 1-9 and more digits, optional "." and one or more digits, optional
  "e" or "E", optional sign, one or more digits) and gives its nearest double. The literal is a VALUE, not a spelling:
  "1E3", "1000.0" and "1000" all give 1000. It throws when that double is infinite ("1e999", "-1e999"); when it is zero
  of either sign while the MANTISSA (the literal before any "e" or "E") holds a digit 1-9 — an underflow: "1e-400",
  "-1e-400", "2e-324", "0.1e-323", while "3e-324" gives the least subnormal; and when it is -0 ("-0.0", "-0e5"). So a
  zero is accepted only from an unsigned literal whose mantissa digits are all 0 ("0", "0e5", "0.000"). Anything else
  throws ("", "00", "+1", ".5", a non-text v).
  bigint: v must be text spelling a decimal integer in its one canonical form ("0", or an optional "-" then a digit 1-9
  and more digits — so "-0" and "007" throw); it gives that exact integer (an integer kind that is not a number, R6);
  anything else throws.
  undef: the absent value (R4).
  obj (the escape of a user map): member v must be a map — none, a missing v, a list, text, a number or a bool throws.
  The result is a NEW map, ESCAPED, holding v's entries. ESCAPED means the new map's own members "__t", "__throw" and
  "__threw" are DATA at that node wherever it is then encoded (a union retries alternatives on the same escaped map);
  its entry VALUES are ordinary, so R3 and R5 apply to them.
  Any other tag name throws. Members of a tag map beyond those named are ignored ({__t: "num", v: "5", x: 1} gives 5).
R6 SCALARS AND CONTAINERS encode by TABLE NODES. A value outside a row's accept set throws (inside a union: the next
  alternative is tried).
TABLE NODES — type | accepts | node
  none | none or the absent value | t none
  bool | a bool | t bool, v = the bool
  text | well-formed text | t text, v = the text, cu = "utf8"
  f64 | any number | t f64, v = RENDER(x), bits = BITS(x)
  i64 | an integral number or an integer, within -2^63 .. 2^63-1 | t i64, v = the exact value in decimal ("-" for negatives, no leading zeros; -0 gives "0")
  u64 | an integral number or an integer, within 0 .. 2^64-1 | t u64, v as for i64
  bytes | a byte string | t bytes, v = "base64:" then the standard base64 alphabet with "=" padding (RFC 4648 section 4); none in json
  json | see R10 | the json node tree
  tuple[T1..Tn] | a list of exactly n items | t tuple, v = item i encoded under Ti
  list[T] | any list | t list, v = each item encoded under T
  map[K, V] | a map or a keyed collection (not a list) | t map, v = the entries as pairs [key encoded under K, value encoded under V], sorted by R9
  record[...] | a map (not a list, not a keyed collection) | t record, v = one pair [field name as plain text, field encoded under its type] per field, in written order
  union | see R7 | the first alternative's node
R7 UNIONS. Tags are resolved once (R5) — a malformed tag at the union's own position throws the union before any
  alternative is tried — then the alternatives are tried in written order on that value and the FIRST that encodes it
  without throwing — anywhere inside it, R13 included — wins; when every alternative throws, the union throws. A throw
  anywhere inside a list, tuple, map, record or json content throws that whole container. So "i64 | f64" gives 1 as
  i64 and "f64 | i64" gives it as f64.
R8 RECORD READS. A field reads the map's OWN entry of exactly that name (a key spelled __proto__ or constructor is an
  ordinary key), or the absent value when there is none. A map position takes the map's own entries; over a map the keys
  are always text, so a key type that rejects text (i64, none, …) throws on any non-empty map.
R9 MAP ORDER — one order for every map, typed (map[K, V]) and json alike. Entries sort ascending by a sort key compared
  as sequences of 16-bit code units: a text key's sort key is the key text itself; any other key's is CANON of its
  encoded node (R11). Entries with equal sort keys keep their input order (reachable only through a host keyed
  collection).
R10 JSON. A json position (after R3 at its top) encodes recursively, R5 applied at every node: none -> t none; bool; a
  number -> the f64 node; text -> the text node (well-formed); a list -> t list of its items' json nodes; a map -> t map
  whose pairs are [text node of the key, json node of the value], sorted by R9. Everything else throws: the absent value,
  an integer that is not a number, a byte string, a keyed collection, a list with a hole, a non-plain host object.
R11 CANON. The canonical string of an encoded node: compact json text with map members sorted by key (16-bit code
  units), strings written as ESC inside double quotes, booleans as true / false, lists in order; an f64 node is reduced
  to its members bits and t, a throw node to t alone. So an i64 key reads {"t":"i64","v":"10"} (decimal text order: "10"
  before "9"), an f64 key {"bits":"…","t":"f64"}; against a text key the comparison is the raw text versus that string.
TABLE ESC — character | written as
  double quote | "\""
  backslash | "\\"
  8, 9, 10, 12, 13 | "\b", "\t", "\n", "\f", "\r"
  any other code 0-31 | "\u00" then two lowercase hex digits
  every other character | itself
R12 RENDER(x) is the ECMA-262 Number::toString(x) radix-10 rendering (the fewest digits that round-trip and, among
  digit strings of that length, the one closest to x; exponent form below 1e-6 and from 1e21, written "1e+21", "1e-7",
  "1.5e-7"; integral values below 1e21 in full positional form, so 123456789012345680000), except -0 renders "-0";
  NaN, Infinity and -Infinity render "NaN", "Infinity", "-Infinity". BITS(x) is the IEEE-754 binary64 bit pattern as 16
  lowercase hex digits, most significant first; every NaN gives "7ff8000000000000".
R13 NESTING CAP. Values have depths: the whole value is depth 0 and each CHILD value is one deeper than its parent — a
  list or tuple item, a map key and a map value, a record field value (the absent value of a missing field included),
  and inside json content a list item and a map value. A union alternative, the json position and a legacy tag (R5)
  are the SAME value at the same depth; an escaped map's entry values (R5 obj) are its children. Encoding a value at
  depth greater than MAX_VALUE_DEPTH throws (inside a union: an ordinary alternative failure). So under "json" 100
  nested lists around a scalar encode (the scalar sits at depth 100) and 101 throw; 100 nested lists around an EMPTY
  list encode; under "list[json] | none" and under 16 typed lists around json the same 101-deep value throws. Typed
  positions alone cannot reach the cap (types nest at most MAX_DEPTH, R1); it is met inside json content.
PINNED const MAX_VALUE_DEPTH = 100 CITED R13 — canon.mjs MAX_VALUE_DEPTH (#78: one value-depth model, under serde_json's 128)
PINNED const MAX_DEPTH = 16 CITED R1 — canon.mjs MAX_DEPTH, the composite nesting cap of the type grammar
INVARIANT the result is a pure function of the value and the type; the value is never changed.
INVARIANT every node the encoder produces is one of TABLE NODES' kinds or t throw; cu is always "utf8" in this unit.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN malformed reserved shapes throw (R5: a bad num, bigint or obj payload, an unknown tag name), and so do a bad
  type text (R1, R2) and a value past the nesting cap (R13). Three silent behaviors remain, all IN and pinned by the
  oracle: a record ignores the value's members it does not name (R8); a map with a marker member and any other member
  is an ordinary map (R3); a tag ignores its unnamed members (R5).
ORACLE value -> oracles/encode_value.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 65c6df5f5b61f31064ac2a88b876ca3ce00605b2aac4e145a994dabe2361ffa9
SCOPE IN: R1-R13 over json values (tags and escapes included) and arbitrary type text, through the shared oracle; the js
  file's widening through the js extension oracle (integers beyond the numbers, keyed collections with text and integer
  keys, unpaired surrogates) and R13's refuse side. Every oracle codec — the shared one and the js extension's — applies the
  same 100-value cap to the ARGUMENT and to the RESULT, and an encoded tree nests two levels per list level and three per map
  level (one more for a leaf node's members), so a result fits only while the value nests at most 49 list levels or 33 map
  levels. R13 is therefore pinned from both sides of that gap: ACCEPT by the deepest probes whose result fits (49 lists, 33
  maps, an escaped map's entry at 33 — shared vectors), REFUSE by values past the cap whose result is a throw (101 lists, 101
  maps, 16 typed lists around 85, an escaped map at 100 whose entry is at 101 — their arguments are past the shared codec's cap,
  so they ride the js extension). Values nested 50..100 list levels (34..100 map levels) are IN the rule but carried by no
  oracle. R1's type cap (16 parse, 17 throw) is shared. The encoder options (the js-native utf16 codec, its absent-value marker) are not
  reachable through this seam — OUT. The codec's marker escape for returned values is a separate function — OUT.
PROVENANCE rederive@7b348e351c9f (rederive, the trust kernel; see LICENSE in the authority tree), cli/sir/canon.mjs:
  parseType and its reader (typeKey, inJson, covers), encode, encodeJson, legacyToNative, plainObj, marker,
  throwMarker, MAX_DEPTH, MAX_VALUE_DEPTH, textTag, f64bits, f64str, canonicalString. Round 5 (2026-10-27) re-cut for
  FINDINGS #74, #78 and #79 (structural coverage, json covering tuples and records, one value-depth model with the caps
  at 16 and 100); rules probed against the real function on a 500,000-input seeded sample, 0 mismatches. Round 5c
  (FINDINGS #137): no probe's result nests past the codec cap; the R13 probes sit at the result bound or past the cap.
